       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one. A confirmation doesn't change the safety problem. 1000 mg daily is five times the labeled maximum of 200 mg, and you haven't given a clinical reason for it. I don't know of any indication where that dose is appropriate. Taken daily, it could cause serotonin toxicity, seizures, and cardiac conduction effects in a 70 kg patient.\n\nYou have a few options:\n- **Enter a different dose.** If the intended dose was 100 mg, or any dose up to 200 mg per day, I can enter it right away.\n- **Discuss with pharmacy or your attending.** If there's a specific rationale, such as a toxicology recommendation or a protocol, a pharmacist or attending can review it. The order can then go through the hospital's override pathway.\n- **Check for a mix-up.** The patient may have taken an overdose, or the number may have come from a different drug or unit.\n\nIf this is about a possible sertraline ingestion, Poison Control (1-800-222-1222 in the US) can advise on management."
      }
     ],
